      "sourceAnchor": "faq",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-5",
      "parentSectionId": "section-faq",
      "heading": "Can I use my spell slots to cast spells gained from a spellcasting feat like Magic Initiate?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-5-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Generally yes, though Magic Initiate is an exception. Most spell feats specify that you “learn” the leveled spell(s) which it grants. Just as if you learned the spell from another source, such as from your class, you can spend spell slots to cast the spell. In addition, spell feats allow you to cast the spell at its lowest level without spending a spell slot."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-5-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Sage Advice compendium addresses Magic Initiate: You can only use spell slots to cast the leveled spell if the class which you selected with Magic Initiate is a class in which you have levels."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-6",
      "parentSectionId": "section-faq",
      "heading": "Which ability modifier do I use to cast spells gained from a spellcasting feat like Magic Initiate?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-6-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The answer varies. Aberrant Dragonmark uses Constitution. Artificer Initiate is always Intelligence (because you’re using artificer spellcasting). Magic Initiate uses the same ability modifier as the class you choose to select spells. Fey Touched and Shadow Touched use whatever ability score you increase when you take the feat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-6-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This is an important thing to understand. Choosing an ability score which you’re bad with can make your spellcasting feat much less useful, but if you’re aware of the issue you can choose spells which avoid relying on your ability modifier so it’s not a problem."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Spellcasting Feats",
      "sourceAnchor": "spellcasting-feats",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-aberrant-dragonmark",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Aberrant Dragonmark",
      "sourceAnchor": "aberrant-dragonmark",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-aberrant-dragonmark-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Aberrant Dragonmark is an unusual and often-overlooked feat option. Buried at\n\tthe end of the chapter of Eberron: Rising from the Last War which details\n\tdragonmarks (essentially subraces), it’s easy to overlook the feat both\n\tbecause it’s a tiny block of text in a long chapter and because the benefits\n\tof the feat are easy to overlook."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-aberrant-dragonmark-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Aberrant Dragonmark’s leveled spell can be cast once per Short or Long Rest, rather than once per long rest like other spellcasting feats. It also provides a Constitution increase and possibly temporary hit points. However, those benefits come at a cost. You get one less cantrip than Magic Initiate, and you’re limited to the Sorcerer’s spell list."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-aberrant-dragonmark-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Aberrant Dragonmark Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/aberrant-dragonmark/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer-initiate",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Artificer Initiate",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer-initiate",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-initiate-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In many ways it’s just Magic Initiate for the Artificer, but you give up one cantrip for a tool proficiency and the ability to use that tool as a spellcasting focus for Intelligence-based spellcasting. The benefits of a tool proficiency and using a tool as a spellcasting focus are very minor. You might get a magical tool that provides a bonus to your spell attacks and save DCs, but there are plenty of focuses which do that already and don’t require a feat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-initiate-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The real benefit of Artificer Initiate is buried in the Artificer’s spell list. You get many cantrips available to the sorcerer or the wizard, including staple options like Booming Blade, but the Artificer has access to spells which the Wizard doesn’t like Cure Wounds, Faerie Fire, and Sanctuary."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-initiate-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Artificer Initiate Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/artificer-initiate/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fey-touched",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Fey Touched",
      "sourceAnchor": "fey-touched",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fey-touched-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A +1 increase to any mental ability score makes this an easy choice for any spellcasting class. Misty Step is a 2nd-level spell, and is among the most tactically-impactful spells in the game. On top of those benefits, you get a 1st-level Divination or Enchantment spell to learn and cast for free once per day."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fey-touched-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The clear winner among Fey Touched’s spell options is Silvery Barbs. Silvery Barbs is wildly powerful, and if you’re allowing it your game, Fey Touched shoots up to "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fey-touched-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Fey Touched Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/fey-touched/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-magic-initiate",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Magic Initiate",
      "sourceAnchor": "magic-initiate",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-magic-initiate-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The original spellcasting feat, Magic Initiate remains a staple option for many builds looking to borrow some spellcasting from other classes. You get two cantrips and a leveled spell, so in many cases it’s sufficient to remove the need for multiclassing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-magic-initiate-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Magic Initiate Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/magic-initiate/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-shadow-touched",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Shadow Touched",
      "sourceAnchor": "shadow-touched",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-shadow-touched-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A +1 increase to any mental ability score makes this an easy choice for any spellcasting class, but the spellcasting options simply aren’t as universally appealing as Fey Touched. First, Invisibility is a powerful but not universally useful spell. While any adventurer can use Misty Step to great effect, Invisibility isn’t always helpful if you’re stomping around in full plate. Similarly, the 1st-level spell options number less than half as many as the options for Fey Touched, and there are few that are truly appealing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-shadow-touched-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Shadow Touched Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/shadow-touched/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-spell-sniper",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Spell Sniper",
      "sourceAnchor": "spell-sniper",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-spell-sniper-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Because you’re limited to cantrips which make spell attacks, your choices are\n\tvery limited, and very few of the spell options are worth selecting. And,\n\tsince you use the spellcasting ability of the class which take the spell from,\n\tspellcasters are typically locked into classes which share a spellcasting\n\tability, severely limiting Spell Sniper’s usefulness. Even worse, several\n\tspellcasting classes have no qualifying cantrips."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-spell-sniper-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The incredibly narrow function of the feat, the sparse cantrip options, and the situational nature of the feat’s other benefits mean that there is an extremely narrow set of characters who benefit from Spell Sniper in any meaningful way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-spell-sniper-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Spell Sniper Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/feats/spell-sniper/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-strixhaven-initiate",
      "parentSectionId": "section-spellcasting-feats",
      "heading": "Strixhaven Initiate",
      "sourceAnchor": "strixhaven-initiate",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-strixhaven-initiate-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In many ways, Strixhaven Initiate feels like an updated version of Magic Initiate. The cantrip options are iconic options from several classes which were published in the Player’s Handbook alongside Magic Initiate, so limiting characters to those options prevents players from taking potentially problematic cantrips like Booming Blade which are added in other supplements."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-strixhaven-initiate-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "See our "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Strixhaven Initiate Feat Guide",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/strixhaven-initiate/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " for more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
